diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist. Direct measurement inside the lab
contradicts every element of the helpdesk ticket. spine_router_0_0 has not
crashed: all five FRR daemons (watchfrr, zebra, mgmtd, bgpd, staticd) are
running, all three of its Ethernet interfaces are in UP/LOWER_UP state, and
it holds three fully-established eBGP sessions — one upstream to
super_spine_router_0 (AS 65000) and one each downstream to leaf_router_0_0
(AS 65200) and leaf_router_0_1 (AS 65201) — with non-zero prefix counts in
both directions. Its routing table contains correct BGP-learned entries for
both the 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 subnets. End-to-end connectivity
between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is fully functional: a
live ping traversed the fabric with 0% packet loss and stable sub-2 ms
RTTs. The routing tables on both leaf routers carry the correct inter-rack
BGP routes, and super_spine_router_0 is healthy too. Even if spine_router_0_0
were to fail, the dc_clos_bgp topology includes a fully redundant path via
spine_router_0_1, so a single spine failure cannot isolate the two racks.
Root cause: the ticket describes a condition (spine crash, full connectivity
loss) that is not present in the network at the time of investigation. No
corrective action is required; the network is operating normally and no
device should be rebooted.
=== END ===
